{0 Support and known issues}

Behavior that differs from JavaScript Workers and native OCaml, and features
not supported yet.

{1 Differences from JavaScript Workers}

- Await only in the supported handler contexts. A function that JavaScript
  calls, such as a [setTimeout] callback, cannot await. See
  {{!page-async}Async and background work} for those contexts.
- {!Workero.URL} uses the WHATWG [URL], but {!Workero.URL.path} is decoded and
  only [http] and [https] URLs parse. See
  {{!page-url}URLs}.
- A response cannot have a status below 200 or above 599. The runtime rejects
  them, so {!Workero.Status} has [`Continue] and [`Switching_Protocols] only to
  read codes; use {!Workero.Websocket} for 101.

{1 Limits of streams, forms and WebSockets}

- Workero has no client-disconnect callback for a streamed response. A
  {!Workero.Stream.Writer.write} can reject when the client disconnects.
- {!Workero.Response.copy} and {!Workero.Response.of_stream} share the original
  body stream. It can be consumed only once. Call {!Workero.Response.clone}
  before reading if you need two copies.
- {!Workero.Request.form_data} holds the whole upload in memory. See
  {{!page-forms}Forms}.

{1 Binding limits}

- {{!page-queue}Queues}: consumers do not receive the content type.
  {!Workero.Queue.Message.body_json} on a [Text] body gives a JSON string.
  The other readers require compatible JavaScript value types and raise
  [Invalid_argument] for incompatible values. A [v8] body can be
  read only through {!Workero.Queue.Message.body_js}; workero producers cannot
  send [v8]. [send] and [send_batch] expose [delay_seconds], but no other send
  options. The runtime checks message size. Configure dead letter queues and
  [max_retries] in [wrangler.toml].
- {{!page-rate_limiter}Rate limiter}: the key is the only call argument. Set
  the limit and period in [wrangler.toml]. Counts are per location and
  approximate.
- {{!page-ai}Workers AI}: streaming answers and gateway options are not
  supported. {!Workero.Ai.run} raises [Invalid_argument] for a stream.
- {{!page-analytics_engine}Analytics Engine}: [write] returns no result. The
  runtime checks the limits of 20 blobs and 20 doubles. There is no query API.
- {{!page-hyperdrive}Hyperdrive}: connection details only. There is no query
  driver, so workero cannot read from the database yet. The host and password
  are valid only inside the running Worker. In [workerd] the host is a fake name
  with no database behind it.
- {{!page-socket}Sockets}: outbound connections only, from a handler. There is
  no [connect] handler for incoming TCP and no [Socket.upgraded].
  {!Workero.Socket.opened} gives no address information. [workerd] cannot test
  TLS: with a Worker as outbound, [secure_transport:On] and [start_tls] fail.
  Cloudflare limits the destination (no Cloudflare addresses, loopback or
  port 25) and the number of open connections for each request; see its
  documentation.
- Sockets: [index.js] always imports [cloudflare:sockets]. It is available on
  Cloudflare and in [workerd] without a flag. A program run outside a Worker,
  for example in Node.js, raises [Failure] from {!Workero.Socket.connect}.
- {{!page-email}Email}: [workerd] has no way to deliver a test email, and it has
  no [cloudflare:email] module. {!Workero.Email.reply} fails there with a
  {!Workero.Js_error}. The tests call the handler from a JavaScript harness with
  a mock message and a stand-in module, so they do not prove what the real
  runtime accepts.
- Email: {!Workero.Email.reply} takes the raw message as a string, not a stream.
  {!Workero.Email.raw_text} and {!Workero.Email.raw_bytes} copy the whole
  message into the memory of the program; use {!Workero.Email.raw} for a large
  message. The raw message can be read once. The runtime checks the rules for
  [forward] headers and for the [reply] message.

{1 Workflow limits}

- {{!page-workflows}Workflows}: workerd has no Workflows engine, so the tests run
  the class and the binding against a small JavaScript engine. It calls the
  OCaml class over RPC as Cloudflare's engine does, and it implements steps,
  retries, [sleep], [wait_for_event] and replay. The tests do not prove
  persistence, eviction, real retry timing, the Cloudflare limits or a
  deployment. Run a Workflow with [wrangler dev] or on Cloudflare before you
  rely on it.
- Step results, event payloads, params and outputs are JSON values; integers
  above 1073741823 do not parse (see {{!page-workflows}Workflows}).
- Not wrapped: [retention], [createBatch] with [count], [deleteBatch],
  [Instance.delete], [subscribe], [terminate] and [restart] options, rollback
  handlers, the step context ([attempt]), a function as [delay], the cron
  [schedule] and the [workflowName] of the event. [wait_for_event] returns the
  payload, not the event.
- A Workflow class and a Durable Object or entrypoint class cannot share a name.

{1 Entrypoint limits}

- {{!page-entrypoints}Entrypoints}: arguments and results are JSON values.
  [Uint8Array], [Date], [Map], [BigInt], streams and stubs do not cross the
  call. The [scheduled], [queue], [email] and [tail] handlers of a class are not
  supported.
- The runtime creates a new instance of an entrypoint for each call, so it keeps
  no state between calls.
- An entrypoint and a Durable Object class cannot share a name.
- A method declared in [~rpc] on a class whose handlers have no [rpc] fails on
  the first call, not at build time.

{1 Durable Object limits}

- The storage API requires a SQLite-backed class. A class listed in
  [new_classes] can build, but its key-value and SQL calls fail at runtime.
  See {{!page-durable_objects}Durable Objects}.
- RPC methods on a stub: names are declared with [register ~rpc]. Arguments and
  results are JSON. [Date], [Map], [BigInt], [Uint8Array], streams, stubs and
  functions cannot cross the call. A method name cannot be [fetch], [alarm],
  [connect], [constructor], [prototype] or a [webSocket*] handler. See
  {{!page-durable_objects}Durable Objects}.
- Stored values are JSON. A [Date] that other code stores reads back as its ISO string and a [Map] as [{}]. A [BigInt] or a cyclic value raises [Invalid_argument].
- The function given to [register] cannot call {!Workero.Promise.await}. Load stored values in [~init]. Keep per-object state in closures.
- {!Workero.Durable_object.register} must run when the module starts. A [register] call that a condition skips is not exported.
- [workero build] needs [workerd]. The main module must be [index.js].
- A [block_concurrency_while] function that raises resets the object.
- SQL: [Int] values are exact only within ±[2^53]; a larger integer can
  read back as a [Float]. A whole-number [REAL] such as [2.0] reads back as
  [Int]. There is no asynchronous transaction. [Sql.run] returns only the
  number of rows written. {!Workero.Durable_object.Storage.transaction_sync}
  rejects {!Workero.Promise.await} with [Invalid_argument] and rolls back.
- {{!page-websockets}WebSockets}: a socket accepted with
  {!Workero.Websocket.accept} does not hibernate. Do not also call
  {!Workero.Durable_object.State.accept_websocket} on that socket. A class
  without [websocket_message] can accept hibernatable sockets, but does not
  handle their messages.
- The [workerd] tests cannot prove eviction and re-creation during hibernation,
  jurisdictions, or the tag and 2048-byte attachment limits. Test these in
  a deployed Worker.
- {{!page-durable_objects}Containers}:
  {!Workero.Durable_object.Container.get_tcp_port} supports HTTP [fetch] only;
  raw TCP [connect], [setInactivityTimeout] and outbound interception are not
  wrapped. The tests run [State.container] against [None] in [workerd] and
  against a JavaScript stub. A separate test, [test/worker-containers], starts
  real containers in CI and prints [SKIP containers] where Docker or its images
  are missing. [workerd] runs containers only with Docker and an egress
  interceptor image.

{1 Not supported yet}

- The [tail] handler.
- Durable Objects without SQLite storage.
- Vectorize and the Send Email binding.
- [HTMLRewriter].
- [AbortController] and request cancellation.
- Most of [crypto.subtle]. {!Workero.Crypto} has digests, HMAC, AES-GCM, PBKDF2
  and HKDF with raw keys. It has no key generation, no [exportKey], no [jwk],
  [pkcs8] or [spki] import, no RSA, ECDSA, ECDH, Ed25519, AES-CBC or AES-CTR, no
  [wrapKey] and no [DigestStream]. Keys are never extractable.

{1 Differences from native OCaml}

- [Sys.word_size] is 32, and [int] has 31 bits.
- There is no file system, except files that you embed. See
  {{!page-libraries}Supported libraries}.
- The program has no threads, sockets or sub-processes.

Successful compilation does not prove that a library's runtime dependencies
are available in a Worker. See {{!page-libraries}Supported libraries} and
{{!page-testing}Testing a worker}.

{1 Report a problem}

Open an issue in the
{{:https://github.com/ml-in-barcelona/workero}workero repository}. Include the
OCaml, wasm_of_ocaml and Wrangler versions, and the smallest worker that shows
the problem.
